ever since razer announced the blade  studio i've been excited since we'll  finally have an ultra premium powerful  Windows laptop focused on professionals  finally something to go head-to-head  with Apple but taking what is good and  then also improving on what's not but  after getting to use it and test it the  blade falls short in four different ways  which I'll talk about throughout this  video there's also a lot of good and for  one particular group of people this  machine will be a godsend  but for others like photo and video  editors I have an alternative Windows  laptop with the same olá display that  will save you over $1,000 I love the  design it's an all aluminum chassis  coming in at a fairly lightweight 4.8  pounds and it's very rigid with aluminum  that doesn't feel cheap like on some  other laptops  I am glad also that Razer made their  logo nice and subtle for this pro  machine the charging port looks like USB  type-c but it's much larger and has a  really tight fit next to that we have a  headphone jack and two USB type 8 ports  that run a full 10 gigabit per second  speeds but that's not all we have  another one on the right-hand side next  to a DisplayPort output HDMI and a  single Thunder both three port I really  don't get why we only have one Thunder  both three port entry-level Windows  laptops are now starting to ship with  two so I think that this is a huge  mistake with a pro machine on the plus  side we do have an SD card reader that  supports ultra-fast UHS type 3 cards  that aren't even available yet but once  they're out those will be able to  transfer it out over 600 megabytes per  second on the inside there's another  feature that my MacBook lacks that is  Wi-Fi 6 so if you have a new router  you'll get faster speeds and better  range the battery is 80 watt hours  better than some of other razors laptops  but about 20% smaller than an XPS 15 or  a MacBook Pro because of this battery  life suffers you can expect about five  hours for lighter tasks whereas the  MacBook gets over 10 hours and for heavy  use like video editing about an hour and  a half compared to over three one  culprit is the gorgeous 4k olla display  it has to push 40  more pixels than the MacBooks 3k screen  and even though OLED could be power  efficient with lower brightness and some  of the pixels turned off when you max it  out it actually draws more power than an  LCD it's also a touchscreen if you care  for that and it has DCI p3 color  accuracy like the Mac but there's a big  caveat to that and I'll cover that a bit  later the chin bezel is quite large  since the 15.6 inch display is sixteen  by nine resulting in about ten percent  less usable workspace compared to a 16  inch 16 by 10 and we miss out on a  fingerprint reader but I absolutely love  the Windows hello login which is so  convenient that I want every laptop to  have it I have one huge issue with the  display and that's reflectivity I love  the fact that it's glossy for sharpness  but even and moderately lit rooms this  gets annoying and even with the screen  maxed out at 400 nits the pure dark  pixel benefits of OLED are gone because  blacks turn gray unless you're doing  most of your work in a dark room a  brighter LCD would have done a better  job the trackpad is surprisingly good  and it's a huge jump forward from the  last razor that I owned  unfortunately the keyboard is really mad  I had to type slower since the keys are  not only fairly shallow but also mushy  which resulted in poor typing accuracy  on the sides is my biggest  disappointment and that's the speakers  [Music]  [Music]  Razer keeps being notorious for poor  speakers and I really hope that they  would step it up on this Pro machine but  instead my iPhone is not only louder but  also sounds better in every way this is  a webcam and microphone quality test  with the Razer Blade 15 studio Edition  this is a webcam and microphone test  with a 16 inch MacBook Pro all right  enough with the negatives let's touch on  what makes this laptop special and  that's the Nvidia r-tx Quadro 5000 razor  doesn't specify it but it is the max  cube version that is rated for 90 watts  of power we have 16 gigabytes of memory  in 3072 quarter cores more than a 20 80  max Q which results in about 35% higher  T flat performance running blenders GPU  benchmark with the classic BMW and  classroom scenes took just 5 minutes and  24 seconds which is just crazy for a  laptop beating out a Vegas 64 and  nearing AMD's current best you've grown  graphics are they could buy which is the  Radeon 7 and both of these will use more  than 250 watts of power instead of just  90 the same thing goes for Unigine  heaven which tests gaming performance  using an extreme preset we got an  average frame rate of 126 believe it or  not that beats out our Mac Pro Vega twos  a hundred and twelve frames per second  that is until we unplug the razor blade  and it no longer has access to 230 watts  of wall power and has to rely on the  battery where the FPS drops from 126  down to 35 almost four times slower than  before and now slower the 'no dell XPS  15 on battery yes we did check all of  the power savings options and found that  razer literally won't let you get out of  balance power mode if you're on battery  just let has it set to medium now other  brands will but the performance will  still go down although some other  laptops will not go down as much as this  one through the blender benchmark and  now it takes 22 and a half minutes  were four times as long and in Geekbench  5s GPU test the score also drops to  about a third on battery now not  everybody needs to be portable so if  you're someone that needs a massively  powerful GPU and you have a wall outlet  everywhere you go you'll be able to make  full use of that Quadro for video  editing if you're doing something very  graphics intensive like exporting Canon  or raw footage in DaVinci Resolve it  will be twice as fast as the best  MacBook Pro but on battery it's twice as  slow a similar thing goes for actual  timeline performance where it can handle  38 frames per second instead of 32 but  unplugged that results in an unusable 13  frames per second now one thing that I  haven't touched on yet is the CPU and  this is where razor made an odd choice  the only option is a 2.6 gigahertz six  core i7 nine Chen and that's a fine  choice for tasks that are mostly GPU  heavy like gaming 3d rendering or apps  like DaVinci Resolve but in other tasks  like photo editing or Premiere Pro that  decision could be a bad one premiere is  very CPU heavy so with that same project  where the razor was twice as fast it's  no longer the case with it being 25%  slower when plugged in and almost twice  as slow on battery for these tasks that  six core CPU is really bada lacking the  graphics card in fact the most GPU usage  that I saw was 28% in Premiere Pro now I  don't want to keep hammering the same  nail here so if you want to see a huge  suite of video editing tasks that  include Final Cut Pro check out my other  video which a link in the video  description for photo editing graphics  makes little difference in what you  mainly need is CPU power so here the six  core is also limiting the performance  because of this if you're somebody that  wants a machine that could be useful for  a wide variety of tasks especially if  you're a photo editor or a use premiere  I would avoid this machine the same  thing goes for those that want to be  able to work without being plugged into  an outlet not only is the battery life  port but also the performance on battery  power as well for those I would highly  suggest getting the Dell XPS 15 which  does have a much weaker graphics card  but one that  throughout all much on battery and the  battery itself is 20% larger the  keyboard speakers and the CPU are better  and the display is the same OLED panel  as well I'll link the $2,600 option in  the description that matches the same  RAM and SSD while having an eye 9 8 core  processor now let's touch on that OLED  display if you're somebody that needs  color accuracy you have to get a display  calibrator and then make sure you  calibrate it at 2 clicks below maximum  brightness because the top levels aren't  capable of the best color accuracy once  you've done that make sure to always use  that brightness setting when you're  doing color critical work because unlike  LCDs this OLED panel shifts colors  really easily when you change brightness  if you're somebody that needs a pro  laptop with the best possible graphics  when working plugged in there is no  doubt that this is a super capable  machine and when you're not pushing the  graphics to the limits it runs fairly  quiet due to that 6 core CPU and nice  cooling design but for most other people  I would look elsewhere especially  considering the price thank you guys for  watching if you like this video click up  there to subscribe and check out our  side-by-side comparison with the MacBook  Pro or the very detailed video editing  vid right over there there's been max  and I'll see you in the next one  [Music]

